        "heading": "2.2 MIXING & MATCHING SCENES (M&MS)",
        "text": "We define an image collage as a list C = [s,O] containing one background scene image, s, and a set of foreground collage elements, O. The set of foreground collage elements is defined as a set of tuples O = {(oc,bc)}Oc=1, where oc is a foreground object image and bc its corresponding bounding box, indicating the location and size of oc in the collage. Consider now a dataset D = {(Ci,xi)}Mi=1 composed of collages Ci and corresponding natural images xi. The objective of collage-based image generation is to model p(x|Ci). In M&Ms, we model p(x|Ci) as p(x|Hi), where Hi is a representation tensor of the image collage Ci. We use a pre-trained neural network f parameterized by \u03d5 to obtain a representation of the background scene image hsi = f\u03d5(si) and of each collage element h o i,c = f\u03d5(oi,c). We then spatially replicate hsi and all h o i,c into Hi using the collage bounding boxes bi,c, as illustrated in Figure 3a. When two bounding boxes overlap, we assume that the smaller box is in front of the bigger one.\nWe train M&Ms adversarially using one generator and two discriminators: a global discriminator and an object discriminator. The objective of the global discriminator is to encourage the generator to produce plausible and appealing images, while the object discriminator ensures that the generated objects are both of high visual quality and consistent with the collage. Our generator G(Zi,Hi) takes as an input a noise tensor Zi and the representation tensor of the collage Hi and outputs a generation x\u0302. The noise tensor is composed of a scene noise vector zs \u223c N (0, I) and O object noise vectors zoc \u223c N (0, I) that are spatially arranged into Zi using the collage bounding boxes bi,c. The generation process is shown in Figure 3b.\nThe design of the training procedure for both discriminators follows the spirit of IC-GAN \u2013 i.e. during training we ensure that the generated images and its objects are within the neighbourhood of xi and oi,c, respectively. We use the dataset D to obtain k nearest neighbour sets Axi and Aoi,c for images xi and object images oi,c, respectively. The neighbours are computed using the image and object representations hxi and h o i,c by means of the cosine similarity, where h x i = f\u03d5(xi). As depicted in Figure 3b, the global discriminator Dg(x,hxi ) discerns between real neighbors sampled from Axi and images generated by conditioning on Hi, while the object discriminator Do(o,hoi,c) discerns between real neighbors sampled from Aoi,c and objects in the generated image, which are extracted using the bounding boxes bi,c. Note that M&Ms reverts to IC-GAN if the image collage only contains a single background scene image.\nTraining M&Ms. The generator and discriminators attempt to find the Nash equilibrium of the following equation:\nmin G max {Dg,Do}\n(1\u2212 \u03bb)Lg(G,Dg) + \u03bbLo(G,Do), (2)\n(a) Process of extracting a collage representation Hi. (b) Training workflow of M&Ms.\nFigure 3: Overview of M&Ms. (a) Example collage Ci with one background scene image, si, and two foreground objects, oi,0 and oi,1. We extract feature representations, hsi , h o i,0, and h o i,1, from the background scene image and the foreground object images, respectively. The object locations and sizes in the collage, bi,0 and bi,1, are used in conjuntion with the extracted feature representations to spatially build the collage representation Hi. (b) We feed the generator G with the extracted collage representation Hi and a noise tensor Zi, and generate an image x\u0302. The global discriminator Dg distinguishes between generated images and real image neighbors, while the object discriminator Do differentiates between generated objects and real object neighbors.\nwhere Lg is the global loss, Lo is the object loss, and \u03bb controls the importance of each term. The global and object losses are defined as:\nLg = E(Ci,xi)\u223cD,xn\u223cAxi [logD g(xn,h x i )] + E(Ci,xi)\u223cD,Zi\u223cN (0,I) [log(1\u2212D g(x\u0302,hxi )] . (3)\nLo = E(Ci,xi)\u223cD,(oi,c,bi,c)\u223cCi,on\u223cAoi,c [ logDo(on,h o i,c) ] + E(Ci,xi)\u223cD,(oi,c,bi,c)\u223cCi,Zi\u223cN (0,I) [ log(1\u2212Do(o\u0302i,c,hoi,c)) ] , (4)\nwhere o\u0302i,c corresponds to the generated object extracted from the generated image x\u0302 using bi,c, and Zi \u223c N (0, I) refers to the sampling procedure of the noise tensor. In practice, obtaining the ground truth collage decompositions of natural images is challenging. However, approximate decompositions can be easily obtained from the xi, by applying a set of class-agnostic bounding boxes to extract object images and assuming that the background scene image si is equivalent to xi. In the absence of class agnostic bounding box annotations, bounding boxes can be obtained by leveraging an object proposal model such as OLN (Kim et al., 2022), as validated in Supplementary Material G.\nM&Ms architecture details. We start from the BigGAN generator and discriminator architectures (Brock et al., 2019) given their ubiquitous and successful use in the literature, and extend them to handle collages. In particular, we modify the normalization layers in the generator to take as input a collage representation Hi (instead of a class embedding) and replace the fully connected layers by 1\u00d71 convolutions. We also adapt the discriminator architecture to operate both at image level and object level, by stacking ResNet blocks which are subsequently fed through an image processing path and an object processing path, tying the parameters of the M&Ms\u2019s image and object discriminators. The image processing path applies a global pooling after the last ResNet block. The object processing path applies a region of interest (RoI) pooling layer (Ren et al., 2015) at four intermediate ResNet block outputs. We then compute the dot product between the output of the image processing path and the real image features hxi to obtain the image discriminator score. Analogously, we compute the dot product between the outputs of the object processing path and the features of the real objects hoi,c to obtain the object discriminator scores. Note that this follows the projection discriminator (Miyato & Koyama, 2018) principle of BigGAN, except that we project on image and object features rather than class embeddings. More details can be found in the Supplementary Material C.2."
